[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65268,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type is a group of primary vulvar tumours showing histopathological features identical to those of breast carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65269,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8500/3 Adenocarcinoma of anogenital mammary-like glands</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65270,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C70.Z &amp; XH9FX2 Malignant neoplasms of vulva, unspecified &amp; Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65271,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> adenocarcinoma of anogenital mammary-like glands (see the WHO classification of skin tumours [[Elder DE, Massi D, Scolyer RA, et al., editors. WHO classification of skin tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2018. (WHO classification of tumours series, 4th ed.; vol. 11). https://publications.iarc.fr/560.]])</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65272,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65273,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common location is the labia majora.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65274,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients are postmenopausal and present with a solitary vulvar mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65275,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are uncommon vulvar neoplasms, typically occurring in multiparous women aged &ge;&nbsp;60&nbsp;years.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65276,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65277,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type is thought to arise from anogenital mammary-like glands {28418164; 27759697}. Mutations in the genes of the PI3K/AKT cascade have been found in a few studied cases. In particular, secretory carcinoma harbours <em>ETV6</em>-<em>NTRK3</em> fusion {28291131; 29672325}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65278,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is that of a solitary nodule with a fibrous cut surface, similar to breast carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65279,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>The most common type is adenocarcinoma identical to mammary invasive ductal carcinoma, uncommonly with an identifiable in&nbsp;situ component. Rare types include lobular, tubulolobular, mixed ductal and lobular, and secretory carcinomas. There is commonly expression of ER, PR, and ERBB2 (HER2) {16625074; 20505503; 16931966}. The lesion must be dermally centred, and there should not be extensive overlying epidermal involvement&nbsp;&ndash; which would instead be classified as invasive Paget disease. On the basis of the immunoprofile, these tumours can be classified, like their breast counterparts, into luminal&nbsp;A, luminal&nbsp;B, ERBB2 (HER2)-enriched, and basal-like subtypes {28418164}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-30T12:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65280,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65281,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65282,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The essential and desirable diagnostic criteria are the same as for the corresponding tumours in the breast.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65283,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM system is applicable as for other vulvar carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65284,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 391,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinomas of mammary gland type are locally aggressive tumours that often metastasize to regional lymph nodes. Distant metastases are relatively uncommon.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:27:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]